            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Dee Joseph"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I sauteed a little onion in butter, rather than oil, and added the matzo brei mixture when the onions were translucent to make a mixture that I enjoyed even more."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "goldenapple82"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is a good base recipe to play with. My husband and I prefer an eggier version, so I use three eggs for two pieces of matzo. \n\nI saute onions in butter, sometimes adding sauteed bell peppers and/mushrooms as well if I have them on hand. \n\nFor a sweet option, I like to peel and cut up apples and cook them in butter with cinnamon before adding the egg and matzo mixture.",
            "datePublished": "2015-04-05T07:32:17.077Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "BigMamaD"
            },
            "reviewBody": "My Dad made this for me as a kid... but he didn't SOAK the matzoh, he \"baptized\" (a little Jewish humour) it by sprinkling. And he used butter. This results in a slightly crunchier matzenbrei. Then he shook grated Parmesan cheese on top, and served it with a glass of milk (\"no milk, no matzoh!\" -- great way to get kids to drink their milk!).\nA half-century later, I still make it this way.",
            "datePublished": "2015-05-06T03:00:42.970Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Terri"
            },
            "reviewBody": "try this with lox and onion. Break up small pieces of nova scotia lox and saute onion in butter or just add to the egg mixture if you like. I run the matzoh over warm water very fast. Use 2-3 pieces and 1-2 eggs for 2 ppl. Delicious!",
            "datePublished": "2010-06-29T10:53:38.160Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "avigee"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is very simple and traditional recipe. I soak in warm water. You have better control. You don't want it to soaky. I like it with just a little firmness. Let the egg mixture soak a little longer to soften completely. I use only oil. The very traditional way is to use schmoltz (rendered chicken fat). My grandmother made it that way. Definitely use a little minced onion. Fry the onion first then add the matzo mixture. This is the Russian way. My wife's family is Polish and they separate the eggs and fold in the whites and serve with powdered sugar.",
            "datePublished": "2010-05-21T09:56:11.600Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Viatroy"
            },
            "reviewBody": "My ultimate comfort food. 2-3 sheets of matzo broken into bite size-ish. cover with warm water, soak for 1 minute or so, then drain water. add 2 eggs to matzo in bowl, mix well. using butter or oil, cook mixture on stovetop, until the egg is cooked throughout. butter, salt, pepper - heaven.",
            "datePublished": "2024-01-26T16:35:08.398Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "stanbldr"
            },
            "reviewBody": "That was the way my mother made it when I was a child. I make it for myself now by running warm water over a couple of sheets of matzoh until I feel they are well soaked. Then break the matzoh into pieces (not too Small) then soak them in 2 scrambled eggs, fry it in butter and I prefer it with maple syrup even though I was brought up having it with sugar.\nIt's a great breakfast.",
            "datePublished": "2014-09-12T00:17:11.290Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Mom Karen"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I soak the matzo pieces in cold milk for at least five minutes (rather than water). I leave out the pepper and fry it in butter, too. With sugar on top it is delicious!",
            "datePublished": "2010-03-23T16:45:11.173Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "joshua"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I follow the recipe to the tea but I add a little bit of sour cream and shredded cheese to make it a little savory",
            "datePublished": "2026-04-02T22:39:52.677Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "RowdyBacon5329"
            },
            "reviewBody": "It was very good except for being a little on the tough side. In some ways, it reminded me of potato pancakes, but it's faster because you don't have to grate the potatoes. The next time I make it, I'll put some onion in the batter and see what happens. I topped the pancakes with apple butter, apple sauce, or cottage cheese. (I often eat matzoes with cottage cheese.)",
            "datePublished": "2025-05-09T21:08:03.255Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Cookie734"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is very close to how my mom made it. I like mine a little bit more \"eggy\" so I will do three or four eggs (beaten with a hand mixer) per recipe. I also use butter instead of oil. Delicious!",
            "datePublished": "2013-03-30T12:02:31.017Z"
